WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.439 --> 00:00:05.590
In the early years of the North American League Championship Series players and organizations

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:10.320
banded together to legitimize competitive
gaming as a mainstream spectacle.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.400
Now, the NA LCS is big business.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:21.320
It costs millions to field a team on that stage, and more than ever the pressure is on players to perform.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.280
Rosters are constantly in flux as teams import international talent in the pursuit of winning

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.380
an NA LCS Championship.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:35.400
Organizations need franchise players that they can build an identity and culture around.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.700
A player to lead their team for years to come.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:45.640
A player who won’t waver when a new patch drops or new players are inserted into the starting lineup.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.760
A player that they can count on to hold a team together.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.780
A player like Sneaky.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.460
"What the f*** is up, professional maid streamer here."

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:29.080
(Casting)

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:39.400
Zachary "Sneaky" Scuderi was born in Winter Springs, Florida, and played competitive World of Warcraft

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.500
before League of Legends was released.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.580
"I grew up in Orlando before I moved out to California to pursue my career."

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.680
"My friends that got me into League, we kinda just played all the games together, we started off with WoW."

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.600
"Which got us into DotA, and that's how I started with Heroes of Newerth, and moved into League of Legends

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.300
through that."

00:01:55.540 --> 00:02:00.180
Sneaky started his competitive LoL career in 2012 with Absolute Gaming

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.000
and like a lot of small tournament teams from that era, they only lasted about four months together.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:13.520
Sneaky then started his own team called Pulse Gaming with Kenikth, Onionbagel, Kevin and Support Daddy.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.620
Pulse, failed to qualify for 2013 LCS summer promotional tournaments at the MLG and IPL

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.620
qualifier events they competed in and as the team constantly shuffled their roster,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:29.760
Sneaky began to lose confidence in their ability to make it to the LCS.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.680
"It's actually really saddening realizing that, because you actually realize that you're not very good as a team."

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.920
"I remember our preparation was like, alright go look up these guys solo queues."

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:45.180
"Whatever, alright let's go. Nothing past that, there was no notebook to write down pick bans, what their plan is."

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.520
"It was just, pick whatever you feel like basically."

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.820
So in April 2013 Sneaky joined Quantic gaming.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.660
Quantic had previously played under the Cloud9 banner without Sneaky during the promotional

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:05.740
tournament for the inaugural LCS split in Spring 2013 but were subsequently dropped by the organization

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.700
after they failed to qualify.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.740
The new Sneaky-empowered Quantic lineup — which also featured Hai, Balls, Meteos and LemonNation

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:22.600
— managed to qualify for the LCS Summer 2013 split and re-joined the Cloud9 organization

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.920
The classic C9 roster, that became one to the cornerstones of the LCS and paved

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.560
the way for the macro gameplay in North America we know today was born.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:38.380
"And they were the most impressive team in the promotion tournament including Dignitas and CLG

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.080
who had been demoted there, they steamrolled compLexity three games to none to earn their spot in the

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:43.800
LCS."

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.840
Cloud9 was nothing short of dominant in their first LCS split.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.400
The team posted a 25-3 record during the regular season and rolled through the playoffs without

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.140
dropping a single match.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:18.200
(Casting)

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:25.420
To this day, that record still puts 2013 C9 as one of the most dominating teams in NA LCS history.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.640
During that first split, Sneaky left his mark on the league.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:39.060
He led all AD Carries with a 6.96 KDA and cleaned up on Ashe, with which he posted a whopping 11-0 record.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:05:04.720
(Casting)

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.120
Cloud9 qualified for the Season 3 World Championship.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:14.520
But despite earning a first-round bye, C9 were eliminated by the tournament’s top EU seed.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:21.840
(Casting)

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:28.400
C9 and Sneaky continued their dominance into 2014 where they once again won the split.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:36.280
And Sneaky once again sat atop the KDA leaderboard not just for ADC but across all five positions.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.320
When you look at the numbers, you could make a case for Sneaky being NA’s best ADC in

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.960
almost any LCS split he’s played in.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.100
"Yeah Sneaky's always been top tier North American AD Carry."

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:52.480
"Sneaky was so consistent for this team, he just shows up every single day."

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.880
Much like C9, he’s the model of consistency.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.740
C9 and Sneaky seemed to understand macro play much quicker than the rest of league

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.660
and Sneaky credits a lot of their success to this.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:18.240
(Casting)

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.180
They were able to play as a team all over the map,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:27.480
and use Hai’s shotcalling and their overall game knowledge to play around Sneaky and snowball games.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:54.220
(Casting)

00:06:54.220 --> 00:07:00.920
Between splits in 2014, C9 attended the international all-star event without their mid laner Hai,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.420
who was sidelined with a collapsed lung.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.520
They went 3-1 in the Group Stage, only losing to Faker’s SK Telecom.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:13.860
They would advance through groups, where they were dispatched by one of the Chinese representatives,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:14.920
OMG Gaming.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.120
(Casting)

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:26.560
Cloud9 put NA on the map, making it clear that the region should not be overlooked internationally

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:34.300
In Summer 2014 Sneaky and Cloud9 were met with real competition for the first time since they’d joined the LCS.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:42.040
"More than ever Cloud9 may not be the first place team. CLG's looking very strong, we also don't know LMQ."

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.760
"How good are they, how bad are they?"

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.800
"Yeah I think the team that has benefitted the most from these roster changes is C9."

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.860
It was clear that the league was shifting as organizations were looking to improve their results

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.080
by importing players and shuffling their rosters.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:03.320
But C9, ever the model of consistency, still fielded the same roster as their inaugural split.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:09.980
Cloud9 finished the split tied for first place with LMQ — an all Chinese imported roster.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:16.160
They then lost the Summer Finals to Team SoloMid who were playing their second split with European

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:17.600
superstar Bjergsen.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:35.520
(Casting)

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.760
After the Summer Finals, it was time for Worlds again. Where Cloud9 would make North American history.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:49.580
Cloud9 went 4-2 in their Worlds Group stage and met Korean powerhouse Samsung Blue in the quarterfinals.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:09:25.120
(Casting)

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:31.840
Cloud9 lost that series 3-1 but became the first NA team in the LCS era to take a game

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.980
off of a Korean team at a major international tournament.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:43.840
Sneaky was a driving force in the series, showing that he could compete alongside game’s best AD Carries

00:09:43.840 --> 00:10:01.680
(Casting)

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.640
Cloud9 rode the momentum they had from Worlds

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:12.040
into another post-season event at IEM San Jose, where Cloud9 and particularly Sneaky shined.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:52.160
(Casting)

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.040
Their bursts of success on the international stage aside, the transition between Summer 2014

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:03.900
and the 2015 LCS season was certainly a changing of the guard for Sneaky and Cloud9.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.400
They simply weren’t the best anymore, Team SoloMid had knocked them off their pedestal

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.640
and they weren’t going to let them take it back anytime soon.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:20.160
Cloud9 went 12-6 in the 2015 Spring Split, rebounding after a shaky first two weeks.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:26.120
They went into the playoffs with a first round bye and were second place overall behind TSM.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.560
But once again, they weren’t able to defeat
Bjergsen and co. in the finals.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:37.700
"Speaking of wins for Team SoloMid, the Nexus falls, and Team SoloMid are the 2015 North American

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:41.200
League of Legends Championship Series Spring Split Champions."

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.000
Despite the veil of dominance being lifted from his team, Sneaky had stayed on course.

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:52.000
He continued to show that he was the ADC to beat in the TSM and C9 rivalry.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:12:11.560
(Casting)

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:18.880
Sneaky once again led all AD carries in KDA, and died less the any other bot lane marksman.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:25.320
Hai was removed from the mid lane prior to the summer season and replaced by Jensen, who was then known as

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:26.320
Incarnati0n,

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:31.920
a young European playmaker whose competitive
lifetime ban was recently lifted.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:38.440
"I don't think the roster change was ever initially brought up by any of the players."

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.320
"I think it was just Jack looking out for us."

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.800
"No one really wanted to go out of our way to say, hey I think we need to change a player out here."

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:52.960
As the 2015 Summer Split started, Cloud9 looked like a shadow of their former self.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:59.640
"The worst trend though was Cloud9 even after beating TSM with the first game of the split,

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:03.300
Now sits at one and three,"

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:14.160
(Casting)

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.500
"And Cloud9 once again find themselves on the short end of the stick here after actually

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:21.020
playing a strong game."

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:25.300
"But not finding the advantage that pushes them to the win just yet."

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:30.160
"There's a lot of growing to do for this new Cloud9 squad with Incarnati0n instead of Hai."

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:36.240
Despite the fact the Sneaky was still making big plays in the bot lane, the team went 6-12

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:42.640
during the split, their worst record ever, and missed the playoffs for the first time in franchise history.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:47.100
"I'd say I definitely have thought of it more about like, not being on Cloud9."

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.600
"Just because we're in such a bad spot right now."

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:57.760
After the split, Sneaky did his best to show that what remained of the old-guard C9 roster could still compete.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:06.720
"Well I think the only ever time that it was talked about leaving, that was kind of a Riot pushed agenda."

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:14.520
"I never really said it. It was just like, "You guys are doing terribly Sneaky, what would think about joining a

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.400
different team." And I'm like, "Yeah we're like 8th place LCS right now.""

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:23.840
They played in a regional finals gauntlet to try and secure the third NA birth in Worlds that year.

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:29.860
C9 was coming off one of their worst LCS performances ever, but the gauntlet gave them a chance

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:33.880
to remain a beacon for North America on the international stage.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:39.320
"To be able to bring it back and actually go to Worlds would be amazing for us considering our

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.320
pretty terrible season."

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:46.580
"As strange as it sounds. Call Cloud9 a Cinderella story, they could go on a miracle run to secure their third

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:48.100
consecutive appearance at Worlds."

00:14:48.160 --> 00:15:20.320
(Casting)

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:25.180
Not only did C9 win the regional qualifier and get a spot at Worlds,

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:27.260
but Sneaky dominated.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:16:04.200
(Casting)

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:13.040
To the surprise of most, Cloud9 dominated the first week of Worlds, going a perfect

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:16.320
3-0 against some of the best teams in the world

00:16:16.420 --> 00:17:05.280
(Casting)

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:12.720
Prior to the 2016 LCS season C9’s persistence to keep much of their lineup together caught up to them.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:18.600
Teams like Immortals were boasting impressive imported talent in the likes of Huni and Reignover

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:22.140
and just being consistent wasn’t going to be enough for C9.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:27.240
Even their biggest rival TSM were making the changes necessary to stay on top,

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:30.820
bringing in Doublelift, Yellowstar and Svenskeren.

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:36.920
C9 made a few changes as well to try to keep up with the quickly shifting NA LCS.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:44.820
LemonNation had retired and C9 added both jungler Rush and support player BunnyFuFu to their roster.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:49.479
Bunny split time with Hai in the bot lane as the organization was trying to reshape

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:51.280
the roster around Sneaky.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:57.040
"Well it's definitely a different atmosphere when we have Bunny in the team because Hai is the super shotcaller

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:02.540
where he's trying to always talk, but when we have Bunny on the team. It's not necessarily a bad thing but he's like

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.860
A bit less talkative, like less commanding."

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:10.380
And then Rush kind of like takes over a little bit of the role, so it's like shared between him and Bunny."

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:15.120
The 2016 Spring Split was just as lacklustre as their Worlds run.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:20.260
They finished third in NA during the split and lost to TSM in the playoffs again.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:25.460
It was at the end of this split that Cloud9 took the rebuilding plan to a new level.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:29.220
They shuffled around their LCS roster with their challenger team.

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:35.340
Impact in the top lane, Meteos in the jungle, Jensen was now full-time in the mid lane,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:42.020
Sneaky at AD Carry as always and Bunny FuFu and newcomer Smoothie split time in the support role.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:47.880
During that year, C9 matched their 12-6 record from spring, making it back to the finals

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:50.680
for the first time in three splits.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:56.700
Sneaky finished second behind Doublelift in KDA and had an impressive split,

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:01.220
acting as a stabilizing presence for a roster that was just learning how to play around each other.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.880
But once again, there they were defeated by TSM.

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:32.060
(Casting)

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:36.880
C9 competed at Worlds again after the season to another quarterfinals exit.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:47.120
(Casting)

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:51.600
They had overhauled the majority of their roster, but still weren’t getting the championship

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.400
results they were looking for.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:59.420
Meteos left the team and Contractz was called up to fill the hole in the Jungle for Spring 2017

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.460
"I think we'll be pretty ready for this spring split, it's pretty important to us."

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:09.680
"I think it's gonna be a pretty big lead into summer and if we can perform now which we should be able to

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:12.200
from our practice then it'll really help us out a lot."

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:19.420
Just as the split was set to begin, Sneaky was the only member of the original Cloud9 left standing.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:24.360
"Does it feel weird thinking about that like, you are the original, you're the only one left."

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:25.440
"Do you get lonely?"

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:28.360
"Yeah, okay I don't know about lonely but."

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:33.960
"I definitely kind of think about that a little bit more as of recently. I was playing against Meteos in scrims.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:37.620
And we were winning one of the games really hard, I started feeling really sad for Meteos,

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:39.540
I was just like, I want to see him do well."

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:45.340
But, even though major changes had finally come to one of the LCS’ original powerhouses,

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:47.060
the results were the same.

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:54.320
This new-look Cloud9 went 14-4 in the regular season, only to lose another playoff final to TSM.

00:20:54.320 --> 00:21:04.800
(Casting)

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:12.200
In a lot of ways both Cloud9 and Sneaky have followed the same enigmatic path through their time in the LCS.

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:16.200
They’re always good, always in the conversation amongst NA’s best.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.700
But it had been a while since they sat atop the standings.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:25.640
Cloud9 went from the shining star of the NA LCS to the leagues to its perennial second-fiddle.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:32.020
They rallied back from a slow start in 2017 summer to make the playoffs, only to be ousted

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.480
in the quarterfinals by Dignitas.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:41.720
Now we’ve recently seen their 2018 run come to an end in similar fashion.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:46.500
They looked impressive for the majority of the first half of the split, but faltered a little

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:49.240
heading into playoffs to finish 12-8.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:55.700
Team Liquid then knocked them out of the playoffs in the quarterfinals for the second time in a row.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:22:01.140
Doublelift getting the better of Sneaky once again, this time in another uniform.

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:07.480
Sneaky had a solid year, but it just hasn’t
been good enough since as far back as Spring 2014.

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.280
"And they're the North American Champions."

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:17.440
Throughout his career with Cloud9 Sneaky’s stream has always been a major part of his

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.060
identity as a player.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:25.040
Just like on the rift, Sneaky’s stream has been consistent ever since he began his career in the LCS.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:29.880
It’s on-stream where he garnered his reputation as a bit of a memer.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:48.840
(Stream)

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:53.300
Sneaky’s always good for a few dozen “feelsgoodmans” a week on stream.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.640
He’s always laid back and joking around with his chat.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:02.620
(Stream)

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:05.040
His streams are just like his playing career.

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:09.220
Not always making headlines, not breaking viewership records.

00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:14.200
But always there, always entertaining and always worth your attention.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:18.200
(Throws pizza)

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.680
Sneaky streams about three or four times a week even during the season.

00:23:22.900 --> 00:23:27.740
While comparable names like Doublelift or Bjergsen can be a little more streaky, sometimes

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.720
taking weeks off at a time.

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:34.680
Sneaky most recently made headlines for his on-stream Lux cosplay,

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:39.360
a long-term sub goal that drew attention from all over the esports world.

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:44.000
Streaming often allows us a little look into the lives of pro players.

00:23:44.080 --> 00:23:48.740
Whether it’s Bjergsen laser focus or Doublelift’s constant trash talk.

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:53.779
The biggest takeaway from what you see from Sneaky on-stream is the the kind of un-shakeable

00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.300
attitude he exudes on the LCS stage.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.640
No matter just how much his chat is flaming him.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:05.440
He’s always got the same nonchalant attitude, and then hits them with a “feelsbadman.”

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:11.100
We’ve arrived in an LCS world where organizations are franchised for the long-term, and consistency

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:15.560
In this new world, Sneaky is the image of
the consummate professional.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:21.100
But amidst another “just okay” split, Sneaky has stood still.

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:25.800
There’s a lot of question marks surrounding
C9 going forward.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.800
But there’s one thing we can likely count on.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:32.140
Sneaky will hold down the bot lane for C9.

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:37.020
And he'll continue to be the cornerstone that holds this iconic franchise together.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:41.520
"I want to keep playing professionally as long as I can. I don't think there's ever a stopping point.

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:44.200
So I'm not going to stop at five years from now, I'm just going to keep on going,

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:45.900
because there's nothing else I'd rather do."

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:55.000
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

